-- title:
--   Two transports on pull-backs give the same G-point
-- statement:
--   Fix primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, a positive integer $N$ not divisible by $r$, and the following arithmetic data: a complete discrete valuation ring $\mathcal O$ of characteristic zero with uniformiser $\pi$, residue field of cardinality $r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$, its fraction field $K_0$, and a domain $\mathrm{Onr}$ over $\mathcal O$ of characteristic zero with an $\mathcal O$-automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, with that ideal maximal, every element integral over $\mathcal O$ modulo it, every monic polynomial of positive degree having a root modulo it, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r$ modulo it. Further: rationals $a,b$ with $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ indefinite and ramified exactly at $r$ and $\bar r$, a maximal order $\Lambda$ containing the rational integers, a coordinate map $\mathrm{coord}$ on $\Lambda$ satisfying `IsOrderCoord`, a fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over the residue ring of $\mathrm{Onr}$ with formal module structure $X_0$ of height $4$ via $\theta_0$, a ring map $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r^2} \to \mathrm{Onr}$, a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ of height $4$ over $\mathrm{Onr}/(r)$, a moduli package $M$ over $\mathrm{Onr}$ which is a Zariski sheaf, a map $\eta$ from rigidified objects to $M$ which (as summarised in the three conjuncts of $h\eta$) separates admissible rigidifications exactly up to isomorphism, is natural in the base, and is locally surjective on a Zariski cover, an embedding $E_0$ of the centraliser of the $\mathbb Z_{r^2}$-action and $\varpi$ on $\Phi$ into $2\times 2$ matrices over $K_0$ with the stated commensurability with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ up to a power of $r$, a reduction map $\kappa$ compatible with the two quotient maps, and an isogeny $\beta_0$ from $\Phi$ to $X_0$ reduced along $\kappa$ of height $4n_0$. Let $B$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\psi : \mathrm{Onr} \to B$, let $x = (E,\rho)$ be a rigidified curve over $(B,\psi)$, and let $L$ be a Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra in which $\pi$ and $r$ are nilpotent. Let $\varphi_1, \varphi_2 : B \to L$ be $\mathcal O$-algebra maps assumed equal. For $i = 1,2$ suppose $x_i$ is a rigidified curve over $(L, \varphi_i \circ \psi)$ together with $g_i : x_i.A \to x.A$ exhibiting $x_i$ as a pull-back of the underlying curve along $\varphi_i$ and with the rigidification of $x_i$ a pull-back of that of $x$ along $\varphi_i$ compatibly with $g_i$, suppose $x_i$ carries a formal module structure $X_i$ via $\theta_i$, and suppose $t_i$ is a rigidification of $\Phi$ over $L$ with $t_i.X = X_i$, with $j_i \le 1$, which is an `IsRigTransport` of $(\theta_0,\kappa,\beta_0)$ through the rigidification of $x_i$ at parity $j_i$, and which is admissible over $\iota$ and the $\mathrm{Fr}^{-j_i}$-twist of $\varphi_i \circ \psi$. Then the two triples $(\mathrm{Fr}^{-j_i}\text{-twist of }\varphi_i\circ\psi,\ \text{nilpotence of } r,\ \eta_L(t_i))$ are equal as points of $\mathrm{GPoint}\ \mathcal O\ M\ L$.
--
--   This is the comparison statement that makes the passage from rigidified fake elliptic curves to Drinfeld's moduli functor independent of the chosen pull-back: pull-backs are formulated relationally, so two pull-backs of one rigidified curve along the same map are distinct objects, and the theorem asserts that the associated $G$-points, including their structure maps twisted by the appropriate power of Frobenius, coincide. It is used in the construction of a natural transport over a Zariski cover, where it supplies both the overlap compatibility and the independence of the cover.
